o advantage.\n- Alternatively, saving the K bomb for later might be prudent, but since I’m the landlord and want to rush opponents, playing aggressively now increases pressure.\n\nHowever, upon closer inspection: the move **four_with_pair: ♣3 ♦3 ♠4 ♦4 ♠K ♥K ♣K ♦K** is invalid because a quad must be accompanied by exactly two single cards OR two pairs — not mixed. Here, ♣3♦3 is a pair, ♠4♦4 is another pair — so this would require the quad to be separate. But no such quad exists here — this move incorrectly combines two pairs and a bomb.\n\nActually, the only true **four_with_pair** listed is:  \n**four_with_pair: ♣3 ♦3 ♠4 ♦4 ♠K ♥K ♣K ♦K** — but this implies the quad is the Ks, and attached cards are the two pairs (3s and 4s). That **is valid**: a bomb of Ks with two attached pairs (3s and 4s). So yes, it\'s legal.\n\nBut wait: do I *have* both pairs?  \n- ♣3 ♦3 → yes  \n- ♠4 ♦4 → yes  \n- ♠K ♥K ♣K ♦K → yes  \n\nSo **this move is valid**: bomb of Ks + pair 3s + pair 4s = quadplex set (four_with_pair).\n\nThis plays 8 cards at once, massive tempo gain.\n\nOther options:\n- Play bomb alone: wastes opportunity to shed more cards.\n- Play triple_with_pair: e.g., Ks + AAs — only 5 cards.\n- Play small sequences: less efficient.\n\nBest move is clearly the **quad with two pairs attached**: use K bomb with 3s and 4s as attachments.\n\nReason: This move plays 8 cards immediately, drastically reducing hand size from 18 to 10. It consumes a powerful bomb but does so productively, avoiding wasting it just to win a trick. Since I\'m the landlord, speed is key. Peasants have 17 each — likely holding weaker cards. Forcing them to pass repeatedly gives me control. This move is unanswerable (only rocket or higher bomb beats it — neither likely), so I will retain lead after this play.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠K", "♥K", "♣K", "♦K", "♣3", "♦3", "♠4", "♦4"]}'}
